WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.000
Every few months, a new AI takes the lead.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.100
Another company.

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.200
Another app.

4
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.500
Another way of working.

5
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.700
And each time, you start over.

6
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.800
With Clavarium, you don't.

7
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.551
You get the best of each AI, in a place that's yours.

8
00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:15.801
The idea is simple.

9
00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:19.801
Instead of moving into each vendor's world, you bring the AIs into yours.

10
00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:22.101
One home, on your own server.

11
00:00:22.201 --> 00:00:25.201
Each assistant gets its own machine, kept apart from the others.

12
00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:28.601
You connect the things you already use, like your mail and your calendar.

13
00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:31.801
And when a job needs it, several AIs can work on it together.

14
00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.583
Whatever you ask, it all happens in that one place.

15
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.797
And in your place, you set the rules.

16
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:38.733
Not the vendor.

17
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:42.333
You decide what each assistant may touch, and how far it may go.

18
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:45.133
You also choose which AI gets your most sensitive work.

19
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.661
Maybe one hosted in Europe.

20
00:00:47.811 --> 00:00:49.129
Because here's the thing.

21
00:00:49.229 --> 00:00:50.411
AI keeps changing.

22
00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:51.515
Your place doesn't have to.

23
00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:55.465
Right now, most of us follow our AI vendor.

24
00:00:55.565 --> 00:00:58.065
When it changes its tools, we relearn them.

25
00:00:58.165 --> 00:01:01.965
When a better AI shows up elsewhere, leaving means starting from scratch.

26
00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:04.572
And when the price changes, we simply go along.

27
00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:07.822
Clavarium doesn't replace those vendors.

28
00:01:07.922 --> 00:01:12.822
It runs their own official apps, signed in with your own account, exactly as they ship them.

29
00:01:12.922 --> 00:01:16.422
It simply gives each one a computer of its own, at your place.

30
00:01:16.522 --> 00:01:17.881
And your files stay with you.

31
00:01:19.031 --> 00:01:21.388
So you always come back to the same place.

32
00:01:21.488 --> 00:01:22.791
The same conversations.

33
00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:23.727
The same files.

34
00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:25.073
The same way of asking.

35
00:01:25.173 --> 00:01:26.631
Type it, or just say it.

36
00:01:26.731 --> 00:01:28.907
From your phone, or from your computer.

37
00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:30.719
Whichever AI is answering behind it.

38
00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:33.069
And that's where it gets interesting.

39
00:01:33.169 --> 00:01:34.669
A better AI comes out?

40
00:01:34.769 --> 00:01:36.869
You switch, in one setting.

41
00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:37.969
Same conversation.

42
00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:39.869
Your files and your reminders come along.

43
00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:40.969
Nothing lost.

44
00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:41.869
Not convinced?

45
00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:43.169
You switch back.

46
00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.869
Vendors can change their terms.

47
00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:45.886
You keep your choice.

48
00:01:47.036 --> 00:01:50.036
Your mail, your files and your calendar stay yours too.

49
00:01:50.136 --> 00:01:52.236
The AI vendor gets no key to them.

50
00:01:52.336 --> 00:01:56.236
The AI only sees what you open to it, and only for the job at hand.

51
00:01:56.336 --> 00:01:58.636
Nothing goes out in your name without your okay.

52
00:01:58.736 --> 00:02:00.174
Or without a rule you set yourself.

53
00:02:01.324 --> 00:02:03.524
That's why one place fits so many lives.

54
00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:05.924
A parent who wants a simple assistant on their phone.

55
00:02:06.024 --> 00:02:09.124
Someone who keeps work and home apart, with mail and meetings sorted.

56
00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:12.324
A developer whose agents fix code, then wait for a yes.

57
00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:14.696
Or someone who just wants to talk to a little box at home.

58
00:02:15.846 --> 00:02:17.288
One thing to be clear about.

59
00:02:17.388 --> 00:02:19.491
Clavarium is free, open-source software.

60
00:02:19.591 --> 00:02:22.046
It's not a service, and not a subscription.

61
00:02:22.146 --> 00:02:25.046
No one sells it to you, and no one runs it for you.

62
00:02:25.146 --> 00:02:27.440
It's shared freely, by volunteers.

63
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.032
You run it wherever you like.

64
00:02:30.132 --> 00:02:32.690
Your own server, or one from any host you choose.

65
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.290
Bring your own AI account.

66
00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.112
Then a single line installs it, on an Ubuntu server.

67
00:02:38.262 --> 00:02:39.462
AI will keep changing.

68
00:02:39.562 --> 00:02:40.503
Your place stays.

69
00:02:40.603 --> 00:02:43.738
And with Clavarium, changing is easy, so you can always use the best of it.

70
00:02:43.838 --> 00:02:45.811
Have a look, at clavarium dot org.
